{"title":"Leg","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>Generally speaking, in math, legs are two sides of right triangles or isosceles triangles.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Right_Triangle\">Right Triangle</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Leg&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Right Triangle\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/0/660dcc149a64cfad6e4739c5b9d0f9a9fc74d42b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] dot((0,0)); dot((4,0)); dot((0,3)); draw((0,0)--(4,0)--(0,3)--cycle); draw(rightanglemark((4,0),(0,0),(0,3))); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"192\" />\nIn a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Right_triangle\" title=\"Right triangle\">right triangle</a>, the <b>legs</b> are the two <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Sides&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Sides (page does not exist)\">sides</a> of the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Triangle\" title=\"Triangle\">triangle</a> that are not the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Hypotenuse\" title=\"Hypotenuse\">hypotenuse</a>. For example, in a 5-12-13 triangle, the sides measuring 5 and 12 are both legs of the triangle.\n</p><p>In a non-equilateral <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Isosceles_triangle\" title=\"Isosceles triangle\">isosceles triangle</a>, the legs are the two congruent sides.\n</p><p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Leg&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018002749\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.011 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.016 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 12/1000000\nPost‐expand in